%% CASE Eduverse University learning platform
Eduverse University is replacing its 15-year-old learning management system with a cloud platform. Two Scrum teams configure and extend the platform, while the migration of 4,000 courses follows a predictive plan tied to the academic calendar.

- The provost wants an **AI tutoring assistant** that answers students' questions using course materials. No university policy on AI yet exists.
- Many **faculty members** are unhappy about the change; some have used the old system for their entire careers.
- Student records contain **sensitive personal data** protected by education privacy law.
- The **cloud vendor** has announced that it will be acquired by a competitor. Contract renewal terms and the product roadmap may change.
@@ BE1 | Hybrid
What should the project manager ensure before the AI tutoring assistant is released to students?
A) Agreed AI governance covering data use, accuracy checks and human oversight
B) A release as early as possible, so that students can benefit and report any problems they find
C) Approval from the developers, since they understand the AI model's behaviour better than anyone
D) A statement on the website that the university is not responsible for any AI answers
= A
? Responsible AI use requires governance: approved data use, accuracy validation, accountability and human oversight. A disclaimer does not replace governance.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
How should the project manager address the faculty's resistance?
A) Involve faculty champions, provide tailored training and show the benefits for their teaching
B) Ask the dean to require all faculty to use the new platform from the first day of the semester
C) Keep the old system available indefinitely for faculty who prefer not to change their courses
D) Avoid engaging faculty until the platform is complete, so that they see only the finished product
= A
? Change succeeds when influential peers champion it and people receive support and see personal benefits. Mandates or indefinite parallel systems undermine adoption.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
What should be done about the student data used by the platform and the AI assistant?
A) A privacy impact assessment with the data protection officer, with controls in the backlog
B) Use the student data freely within the university, since the institution already owns the records
C) Remove all personal data from the platform, even though teachers need it to run their courses
D) Ask the cloud vendor to take full responsibility for privacy compliance on the university's behalf
= A
? Privacy law applies to the university as data controller. A privacy impact assessment identifies required controls, which become part of the work.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
How should the project manager respond to the vendor's acquisition?
A) Analyse contract protections and plan responses such as guarantees and an exit plan
B) Ignore it, since acquisitions rarely affect the products that the acquired company sells
C) Cancel the project immediately and look for a different platform before the deal is closed
D) Wait until the acquisition is complete and then decide whether any action is needed
= A
? The acquisition creates uncertainty about terms and roadmap. Proactive analysis and responses — contract protections, data portability, an exit plan — reduce exposure without overreacting.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Predictive
A transport infrastructure project involves five contractors whose work must be integrated. Disputes arise about who decides on interface issues. What should the governance framework define?
A) Who decides interface issues and how disputes are escalated
B) The names of the contractors' site managers and their mobile phone numbers
C) The daily working hours of each contractor's crews on the construction site
D) The colours of the safety vests that each contractor's workers should wear
= A
? Governance assigns decision rights and escalation paths, especially where several parties' work interacts.

@@ BE1 | Agile
An organisation funds its product teams quarterly. What is the most appropriate governance mechanism for deciding whether a product continues to receive funding?
A) A quarterly business review of outcomes against the product goals
B) A review of the detailed task list to confirm every task was completed on time
C) An annual audit of the team's timesheets and individual productivity metrics
D) Automatic renewal, since agile teams should not be subject to funding reviews
= A
? Value-based governance reviews outcomes regularly and decides on continued investment based on evidence.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
A team leader asks what to do when a decision is beyond the project manager's authority and the sponsor cannot be reached. Where should the answer be documented?
A) In the governance framework, which defines escalation paths
B) In the lessons learned register from a previous, similar project
C) In the team charter, which covers the team's working agreements
D) In the risk register, under the risk of slow decision-making
= A
? Escalation paths and delegated authorities are part of project governance.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
The project charter states that the design phase may close only when the customer has approved the final drawings. What does this condition represent?
A) An exit criterion used at the phase gate
B) An assumption recorded in the assumption log
C) A constraint on the procurement strategy
D) A risk trigger for the design phase
= A
? Exit criteria define the conditions that must be met to close a phase or project and are checked at the phase gate.

@@ BE1 | Agile | multi
Which two responsibilities typically belong to the project sponsor? (Choose two.)
A) Securing funding and resources for the project
B) Resolving escalated issues beyond the project manager's authority
C) Writing the user stories in the product backlog
D) Facilitating the daily scrum
E) Assigning tasks to individual developers
= A, B
? Sponsors provide resources and make decisions beyond the PM's authority. Writing stories belongs to the product owner; facilitation and task selection belong to the team.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
During production of a clinical trial batch, an operator deviates from the approved procedure. The batch may still be acceptable. What should happen?
A) Document the deviation, investigate it and decide on corrective action
B) Continue production, since the batch may still be acceptable to the customer
C) Destroy the batch immediately without recording what happened during production
D) Ask the operator to correct the batch record so that it matches the approved procedure
= A
? Regulated environments require documented deviations, investigation and CAPA. Altering records would be falsification.

@@ BE2 | Agile
A developer adds an open-source library whose licence requires the product's source code to be published if it is distributed. The product is proprietary. What should the team do?
A) Check the obligations with legal experts and replace the library if needed
B) Use the library, since open-source software is free to use in any commercial product
C) Remove the licence file from the library so that the obligations no longer apply
D) Release the product first and deal with any licensing questions only if they arise
= A
? Licence compliance is a legal obligation. Expert review comes first; removing licence notices would be unlawful.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
An energy project's permit requires an environmental impact assessment before construction can start. The software for the control systems is being developed in sprints. What should the project manager do?
A) Plan the assessment before construction while software work continues
B) Start construction and complete the assessment in parallel to save time
C) Delay all work, including software development, until the assessment is complete
D) Ask the regulator to waive the assessment because the software is agile
= A
? Regulatory prerequisites constrain the work they apply to. Other work that is not constrained, such as software development, can continue.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
The project manager sees a worker on site without the mandatory protective equipment. What should the project manager do?
A) Stop the unsafe work immediately and enforce the safety rules
B) Mention it to the site supervisor at the end of the working week
C) Take a photo of the worker to include in the monthly safety report
D) Let the work continue, since the task will be finished very quickly
= A
? Safety compliance requires immediate action when people are at risk.

@@ BE2 | Agile
The product will process card payments, which must comply with an industry security standard. How should the team handle this?
A) Involve security experts to turn the standard into backlog and DoD criteria
B) Ask the payment provider to handle all compliance so the team can ignore the standard
C) Plan a single compliance review after the final release has gone live
D) Treat the standard as a guideline that can be relaxed if the schedule is tight
= A
? Industry standards that apply to the product must be built in throughout, with expert support, rather than checked once at the end.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
A change request will save money but may increase the risk of late delivery. What should the impact analysis include?
A) The effects on cost, schedule, quality, risk and other areas
B) Only the cost savings, since the change was proposed to save money
C) Only the schedule impact, since late delivery is the main concern
D) The opinion of the person who proposed the change
= A
? Integrated change control evaluates all affected dimensions so that decision-makers see the full picture.

@@ BE3 | Agile
Halfway through a sprint, a stakeholder asks the team to switch to a completely different goal because of a new business opportunity. What should happen?
A) The product owner assesses it and, if the sprint goal is obsolete, may cancel the sprint
B) The developers switch to the new goal immediately to support the business opportunity
C) The scrum master rejects the request, since sprints can never be changed
D) The stakeholder adds the new work directly to the sprint backlog
= A
? Only the product owner can cancel a sprint, typically when the sprint goal becomes obsolete. Otherwise new work goes to the product backlog.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
In a hybrid project, the integration milestone with the hardware vendor is baselined. The agile team proposes moving it four weeks later to add more features. What should happen?
A) Submit a change request for the milestone to the change control board
B) Move the milestone, since the agile team controls the release dates
C) Ask the vendor to agree informally and update the plan later
D) Keep the milestone and drop all remaining features without discussion
= A
? Baselined, contract-related milestones change only through formal change control, with impact assessment.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
A change request in a pharmaceutical project affects a validated computer system. What must the impact assessment include?
A) The need for revalidation, with its cost and time
B) Only the cost of the software changes themselves
C) Only the opinion of the system's main users
D) Nothing extra, since the system is already validated
= A
? Changes to validated systems in regulated environments usually require revalidation, which must be included in the impact on scope, cost and schedule.

@@ BE3 | Agile
During a sprint review, a user asks for a small change to the wording on a screen. What should happen?
A) The product owner adds it to the backlog for prioritisation
B) A developer changes the wording immediately during the review
C) The request is submitted to the change control board
D) The request is rejected because the sprint is complete
= A
? Feedback from the review becomes backlog items that the product owner prioritises.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
A team member implemented an unapproved design change that turned out to improve performance. What should the project manager do?
A) Process it through change control and remind the team of the process
B) Accept it without documentation, since the result is clearly beneficial
C) Reverse the change immediately, even though it improves the performance
D) Reward the team member publicly to encourage similar initiatives in future
= A
? Even beneficial changes must be controlled, since undocumented changes create configuration and risk problems.

@@ BE4 | Agile
The team cannot release because the legal department has not reviewed the new terms of service for three weeks. What should the scrum master do?
A) Work with legal to agree a review date, escalating if needed
B) Release the product without the new terms of service and add them in a later release
C) Ask the developers to write the terms of service themselves to save time
D) Accept the delay and reduce the team's sprint commitments until legal responds
= A
? Organisational impediments are removed by engaging the responsible party and escalating when necessary.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
A critical machine on the production line breaks down, halting the installation work. What should the project manager do first?
A) Log the issue, assign an owner and assess its impact and options
B) Order a new machine immediately at whatever price is required
C) Inform the customer that the project will be delayed by one month
D) Blame the maintenance team in the next status report
= A
? Issues are logged, owned and analysed before decisions about replacements or communications are made.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
Developers are repeatedly pulled away to fix production incidents in other systems, disrupting their sprint work. What should the project manager do?
A) Make the impact visible and agree how operational work is allocated
B) Ask the developers to ignore all incident requests until the release is finished
C) Ask the developers to work extra hours to compensate for the time lost
D) Reduce the sprint length so that interruptions have less effect on the plan
= A
? Making the impediment visible and negotiating an explicit allocation (for example, a support rotation) addresses the root cause.

@@ BE4 | Agile
An impediment has been visible on the team's board for three weeks without progress, because it requires a decision by the head of IT. What should the scrum master do?
A) Escalate it to the head of IT with its impact and the options available
B) Remove the card from the board, since it is clearly out of the team's control
C) Keep it on the board and wait for the head of IT to notice it
D) Ask the team to find a way around the decision without informing anyone
= A
? Impediments beyond the scrum master's authority should be escalated to the decision-maker with evidence and options.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
The issue log contains eight open issues. Which issue should the project manager address first?
A) The one with the greatest impact on objectives and urgency
B) The one that was logged first, to be fair to the people who raised it
C) The one that is easiest to resolve, to show stakeholders quick progress
D) The one raised by the most senior stakeholder on the project
= A
? Issues are prioritised by impact and urgency, not by order of arrival, ease or seniority.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid | multi
An issue cannot be resolved within the project team. Which two actions are appropriate? (Choose two.)
A) Escalate it according to the governance framework, presenting options
B) Keep the issue log updated with the owner, actions and status
C) Close the issue, since the team cannot resolve it
D) Ask a team member to resolve it informally outside the process
E) Wait until the next phase gate to mention it
= A, B
? Unresolved issues are escalated through governance and tracked in the issue log until closure.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
Several risks in the register have responses but no defined triggers, so nobody knows when to act. What should the project manager do?
A) Work with risk owners to define trigger conditions for each response
B) Remove the risks without triggers from the register to keep it current
C) Implement all of the contingency plans immediately as a precaution
D) Ask the sponsor to monitor all of the risks personally each week
= A
? Triggers signal when to execute contingent responses; risk owners should define and monitor them.

@@ BE5 | Agile
The team is uncertain whether its chosen architecture will scale to the expected number of users. What should it do?
A) Run an architectural spike early to test scalability
B) Build all features first and test performance at the end
C) Add more servers to the budget in case of problems
D) Ask the product owner to accept the risk without analysis
= A
? Early spikes reduce technical uncertainty when changing direction is still cheap.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
The analysis shows that overall project risk is very high because of several interacting uncertainties. Which response is most appropriate at the project level?
A) Consider re-scoping or phasing the project to reduce overall exposure
B) Address each individual risk separately and ignore their interactions
C) Proceed unchanged, since overall risk cannot be managed
D) Increase the management reserve and stop analysing risks
= A
? Overall project risk can be addressed with project-level responses such as changing scope, phasing or approach, not just individual risk responses.

@@ BE5 | Predictive | fill
A company is deciding whether to launch a product now. There is a 70% chance of a $300,000 profit and a 30% chance of a $100,000 loss. What is the expected monetary value of launching? (Answer in dollars.)
= $180,000
? EMV = 0.70 × 300,000 + 0.30 × (−100,000) = 210,000 − 30,000 = $180,000.

@@ BE5 | Agile
How can an agile team show stakeholders whether risk exposure is decreasing over time?
A) A risk burndown chart of total exposure per iteration
B) The team's velocity chart for the last six sprints
C) A list of all risks ever identified, without status
D) The number of user stories in the backlog
= A
? A risk burndown chart plots total risk exposure over time, making reductions visible.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
An unexpected supplier strike stops deliveries. The risk was never identified, but the team quickly arranges deliveries from a distributor. What is this response called?
A) A workaround
B) A contingency plan
C) A fallback plan
D) Risk transfer
= A
? Workarounds are unplanned responses to events that occur; contingency and fallback plans are prepared in advance.

@@ BE6 | Agile
The team's estimates have been inconsistent, causing unreliable sprint plans. In the retrospective, what improvement could the team try?
A) Use reference stories to calibrate relative estimates
B) Stop estimating and accept all items into every sprint
C) Ask the scrum master to estimate all stories alone
D) Convert all estimates to hours and track them per developer
= A
? Reference stories give the team a shared baseline for relative sizing, improving consistency.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
At project closure, what should happen to the lessons learned so that other projects benefit?
A) They are transferred to the organisation's lessons learned repository
B) They stay in the project files that only the team can access
C) They are sent to the sponsor and then deleted from the project files
D) They are summarised in the final invoice to the customer
= A
? Transferring lessons to the organisational repository makes them available as organisational process assets.

@@ BE6 | Hybrid
Work moves through design (predictive), development (agile) and deployment (predictive), and handovers between them cause long delays. Which technique would best reveal where time is lost?
A) Value stream mapping of the end-to-end flow
B) A stakeholder engagement assessment matrix
C) A resource breakdown structure
D) A requirements traceability matrix
= A
? Value stream mapping shows each step and the waiting time between steps, exposing delays at handovers.

@@ BE6 | Agile
An improvement experiment from the last retrospective made the team's work slower. What should the team do?
A) Learn from it, stop the experiment and try another approach
B) Continue the experiment, since abandoning it would admit failure
C) Stop running improvement experiments, since they create risk
D) Ask management to decide on all future process improvements
= A
? Experiments are meant to provide learning; results — good or bad — guide the next step.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
A construction project builds 12 identical floors. How can continuous improvement be applied?
A) Review each floor and apply improvements to the next one
B) Review the process only after all 12 floors have been completed
C) Keep the process unchanged to ensure all floors are identical
D) Ask the subcontractors to improve their methods on their own
= A
? Repetitive work allows frequent learning cycles; lessons from each floor can improve the next.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
Before rolling out a new procurement process across the organisation, the project manager wants to know how prepared the departments are. What should be done?
A) A change readiness assessment
B) A procurement audit
C) A make-or-buy analysis
D) A Monte Carlo simulation
= A
? A readiness assessment evaluates awareness, capability, culture and support for the change before rollout.

@@ BE7 | Agile
A new collaboration tool will be introduced across 12 departments. What role can change agents in each department play?
A) Champion the tool, support colleagues and report issues
B) Enforce compliance and report colleagues who do not use the tool
C) Replace the project team in configuring the tool
D) Decide whether their department will adopt the tool
= A
? Change agents promote and support adoption locally and provide feedback that helps the project adjust.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
The first two departments have adopted the new system successfully, while others remain sceptical. According to Kotter, what should the project do?
A) Publicise these short-term wins to build momentum
B) Keep the successes quiet to avoid upsetting others
C) Pause the rollout until all departments are convinced
D) Anchor the change in the culture immediately
= A
? Generating and communicating short-term wins builds credibility and momentum for the wider change.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
Employees resist an automation project because they fear losing their jobs. What should the project manager do?
A) Communicate honestly with HR and management about impacts and support
B) Assure employees that no jobs will change, without checking with management first
C) Avoid the topic, since job decisions are not the project's responsibility
D) Speed up the rollout so that resistance has less time to grow
= A
? Honest, coordinated communication about impacts and support addresses fears; false reassurance destroys trust.

@@ BE7 | Agile
How can the team check whether users are actually adopting the newly released features?
A) Monitor usage analytics and user feedback after releases
B) Count the number of features released per quarter
C) Ask the developers whether they think the features are used
D) Assume adoption, since the features met acceptance criteria
= A
? Adoption is measured by actual usage and feedback, which inform further changes and support.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
A new carbon tax will significantly increase the operating cost of the plant the project is building. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact on the business case and present options
B) Ignore the tax, since operating costs are not part of the project budget
C) Redesign the plant immediately without consulting the sponsor
D) Delay construction until the tax is repealed
= A
? External changes affecting the product's life-cycle value should be analysed and brought to decision-makers.

@@ BE8 | Agile
The cloud provider announces that a service the product relies on will be discontinued in nine months. What should the product owner do?
A) Add a migration to the backlog, prioritised to finish in time
B) Continue as planned and deal with the change when the service stops
C) Stop all feature development immediately to start the migration
D) Ask the provider to keep the service running for this product only
= A
? Technology changes in the environment create necessary work; prioritising it in time avoids disruption without abandoning other value.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
A national election may bring a new government that has promised stricter data regulations. What should the project manager do?
A) Monitor it and prepare scenarios for possible regulatory changes
B) Stop the project until after the election results are known
C) Assume the regulations will not change and continue as planned
D) Lobby the government on behalf of the project to prevent changes
= A
? Scenario planning and monitoring prepare the project for possible external changes without overreacting.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
A shortage of qualified electricians in the region is driving wages up and making recruitment difficult. What should the project manager do?
A) Treat it as a risk and plan responses such as early contracting
B) Wait until the electrical work starts and then look for electricians
C) Remove the electrical work from the project scope to avoid the problem
D) Lower the qualification requirements so that more electricians qualify
= A
? Labour market changes are external risks; early responses can secure capacity. Lowering safety qualifications is unacceptable.

@@ BE8 | Agile
Market research shows that customers are rapidly shifting from desktop to mobile use. What should the product owner do?
A) Reprioritise the backlog toward mobile capabilities
B) Continue the desktop roadmap as approved
C) Cancel the product and start a new mobile one
D) Ask the team to build both versions at full speed
= A
? Adaptive planning responds to market changes by reprioritising work toward the highest value.
